  lessonId: civil-craft-moving-load-lesson-v1
  lessonTitle: Moving Load
  lessonImage: {fileID: 0}
  popupDescription: "A <b><#E09500>Moving Load</color></b> is a load that changes position along a structure, such as a vehicle traveling across a bridge.\n\nKey points:\n- Moving loads change position during a crossing.\n- The forces and stresses in a bridge can change as a vehicle moves.\n- Different vehicle positions can produce different bending moments and support reactions.\n- A bridge must remain safe throughout the entire vehicle crossing.\n\nThis lesson has been added to your <b><#E09500>Almanac</color></b>."
  almanacImage: {fileID: 0}
  lessonDescription: "A <b><#E09500>Moving Load</color></b> is a load that <b><#E09500>changes position along a structure</color></b>, such as a vehicle traveling across a bridge. Unlike a load that remains in one place, a vehicle applies its weight at different positions as it moves.\n\nThe <b><#E09500>forces and stresses</color></b> in the bridge can change throughout a crossing. Different vehicle positions can produce different <b><#E09500>bending moments and support reactions</color></b>. For a simply supported bridge, the support closest to a vehicle generally carries a greater share of that vehicle's weight when it is near one end. The bending effect near the center generally increases as the vehicle approaches the middle.\n\nA bridge must remain safe <b><#E09500>from one end of the crossing to the other</color></b>, not just at the starting point or middle. Testing a vehicle over the entire span lets you observe how the structural response changes with its position.\n\nBrooks's market connection must safely carry both taxis and heavier delivery vehicles. Watch how the bridge responds as each vehicle enters, travels along the span, and approaches the opposite side. Continue observing until each vehicle has completely crossed."
